\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Thu Lao (ISO 639-3 tyl; autonym La H\"u, ``black earth'') is a Tai language spoken in villages of Muong Khuong and Si Ma Cai districts, Lao Cai Province, Vietnam. Glottolog treats the tyl entry as the Vietnamese-side form of Dai Zhuang (zhd), spoken mainly in Yunnan, China, and lists it as retired. The ``Bookkeeping'' family label in the task description is a Glottolog placeholder, not a real family. Joshua Project gives a population of about 200 for Thu Lao in Vietnam. Little material exists.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
None found.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Glottolog entry for tyl: \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/iso/tyl}
\item Wikipedia article on Dai Zhuang, which includes the Vietnamese Thu Lao villages: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dai_Zhuang_language}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project page for Thu Lao in Vietnam (scripture translation listed as started): \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/19581}
\item Lao Cai newspaper article (in Vietnamese) on Thu Lao folk songs, noting that young people show little interest while some elders preserve them: \url{https://baolaocai.vn/giu-dieu-dan-ca-thu-lao-post886094.html}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None found beyond the article above.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Thu Lao is not known to be taught. A 2025 report says Vietnam plans to introduce the Lao language in border-area schools, but that concerns standard Lao and not Thu Lao: \url{https://www.tourismlaos.org/2025/06/16/vietnam-to-introduce-lao-language-in-general-education-near-border-areas/}. Dai Zhuang materials from China might be relevant, but none were found in this search.
\end{document}
